Polymorphism is a fundamental concept in object-oriented programming that allows objects of different classes to be treated as objects of a common parent class. It enables a single interface to be used to represent different types of objects, providing flexibility and extensibility in code design.

In PascalABC.NET, polymorphism is achieved through inheritance and method overriding. When a derived class inherits from a base class, it can define its own implementation of a method already present in the base class. The derived class can override the base class method using the `override` keyword. This allows different objects, even of different classes, to respond differently to the same method call.

Here's an example in PascalABC.NET to demonstrate polymorphism:

```csharp
using System;

class Animal
{
    public virtual void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("The animal makes a sound.");
    }
}

class Cat : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("The cat says meow.");
    }
}

class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void MakeSound()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("The dog says woof.");
    }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Animal animal1 = new Animal();
        Animal animal2 = new Cat();
        Animal animal3 = new Dog();

        animal1.MakeSound();
        animal2.MakeSound();
        animal3.MakeSound();
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a base class `Animal` with a virtual method `MakeSound()`. The derived classes `Cat` and `Dog` override this method with their own implementations. In the `Main()` method, we create objects of type `Animal`, `Cat`, and `Dog`. Even though they are declared as `Animal` type, polymorphism allows us to call the `MakeSound()` method specific to the actual object type.

